---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Peloton Treadmill Buyer’s Guide (2026): Tread Flex, Vision and Tread+";
const description =
  "Peloton’s three new Treads checked against its own pages and independent coverage: delivered prices, All-Access, Just Run, the 2021 recalls, space needs and used buys.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "nordictrack-commercial-2450",
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 2450",
    brand: "NordicTrack",
    category: "Closest on Amazon to the Tread Flex: a folding treadmill with a class screen",
    badge: "Closest on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D5YKDXDV?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31NVjoFCMQL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $2,299 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":2299,"high":2299},
    specs: "22 x 60 in. belt · 4.25 CHP (NordicTrack rating) · 0–14 mph · -3% to 12% incline · 400 lb max user · folds",
    pros: [
      "A 400 lb maximum user weight and a 22-inch belt, by NordicTrack’s figures",
      "A 4.25 CHP motor and a 14 mph top speed",
      "A decline to -3% as well as a 12% incline",
      "A 24-inch touchscreen for iFIT classes",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The touchscreen is designed around an iFIT Pro membership at $39 a month",
      "A screen and software that will date sooner than the frame and motor",
      "NordicTrack’s motor warranty terms vary by listing; check them before ordering",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The Tread Flex itself is sold by Peloton, not on Amazon. If you want a folding treadmill with a big class screen from Amazon, this is the nearest, with iFIT classes instead of Peloton’s.",
    description: "The Tread Flex is sold on Peloton's own site, not on Amazon, so this card shows the nearest machine Amazon does sell: a folding treadmill built around a large class screen and a paid membership, as the Flex is. The Commercial 2450 is NordicTrack's folding flagship. NordicTrack lists a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP motor by its own continuous rating and a 400 lb maximum user weight, and the Amazon listing gives a 0–14 mph speed range, a -3% to 12% incline, RunFlex cushioning, a 24-inch touchscreen that tilts and pivots, and a folding frame with assisted lowering. Against the Flex it is faster, wider, rated for heavier users and adds decline; what it does not have is Peloton's instructors, because its classes come from iFIT, whose Pro membership iFIT listed at $39 a month in November 2026. The treadmill runs without the membership, in manual mode. If Peloton's classes are the reason you are reading this page, you can also run the Peloton App on a tablet on any treadmill, including this one. Amazon listed it at $2,299 on 6 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "peloton-tread-vision",
    name: "Peloton Tread Vision",
    brand: "Peloton",
    category: "Standard Belt, Movement-Tracking Camera",
    badge: "Most Buyers",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HDRFM27D?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31jcwMrxC5L._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$3,495 at Peloton on 20 Oct 2026",
    priceBounds: {"low":3495,"high":3495},
    specs: "59 x 20 in. belt · 3 HP · 0-12.5 mph · 300 lb max user · 23.8 in. swivel screen · 68 x 33 in. footprint",
    pros: [
      "A movement-tracking camera for Run Analysis, which scores cadence, stride length and vertical movement",
      "The 23.8-inch swivel screen, built-in fan and front speakers with a rear woofer",
      "Peloton and a major UK retailer agree on a 3 HP motor and a 300 lb (136 kg) user limit",
      "Expert assembly listed at $150, or free delivery if you build it yourself",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Does not fold",
      "Peloton’s own pages give conflicting warranty terms, from 12 months to five years depending on the component and the page",
      "A 6 ft 4 in height limit and 300 lb weight limit rule out some buyers",
      "Only on sale since 1 October 2026, so there is no long-term reliability record yet",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The default Peloton treadmill: buy it if you know you want Peloton classes and form feedback and will keep paying for the membership.",
    description: "The Tread Vision replaces the Cross Training Tread and is the model most Peloton buyers will end up with. Peloton and John Lewis’s listing agree on a 59 by 20-inch (150 by 51 cm) belt, a 3 HP motor, a top speed of 12.5 mph, a 300 lb (136 kg) maximum user weight, a 68 by 33-inch footprint and a 23.8-inch swivel touchscreen. Peloton and The Gadgeteer give 0 to 12.5% incline; John Lewis’s UK listing says 0 to 12%, so incline is left off the specifications line. Peloton lists a machine weight of 316 lb, an 8-inch step-up height and a new cushioning system. Its movement-tracking camera powers Run Analysis and strength-class form feedback. Peloton showed it at $3,495 with the All-Access Membership sold separately.",
  },
  {
    id: "sole-c80-slat-alternative",
    name: "Sole C80 Curved Manual Treadmill",
    brand: "Sole",
    category: "Slat-Belt Alternative to the Tread+ Vision",
    badge: "Closest on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H2KC6YL5?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31bmqOOpE8L._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":333},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$2,399.99 at Sole on 5 Nov 2026; check Amazon on the day",
    priceBounds: {"low":2399.99,"high":2399.99},
    specs: "62 x 19 in. slat belt · 8 magnetic resistance levels · 330 lb capacity · lifetime frame warranty · no motor",
    pros: [
      "A slat belt you drive yourself, like the Tread+ Vision’s Free Mode, for about a third of the price",
      "Eight levels of magnetic resistance for sled-style pushes, the job of the Tread+ Sled Mode",
      "Self-powered, so it needs no circuit and has no drive motor to fail",
      "Lifetime frame warranty",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No motor, so no powered walking, incline or Peloton-controlled classes; it is only ever Free Mode",
      "No screen or camera: classes would come from the Peloton App on your own device, which cannot control it",
      "Only one year on the deck, electronics, parts and labour on Sole’s current listing",
      "Does not fold, and Sole lists it at 261 lb",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For runners drawn to the Tread+ for its slat belt and Free Mode rather than for Peloton itself. If you want the motor, screen and camera, the Tread+ Vision is sold only by Peloton.",
    description: "The Tread+ Vision is sold only by Peloton, not on Amazon, and Amazon sells no motorised slat-belt treadmill, so this card shows the nearest Amazon machine for the part of the Tread+ that has no equivalent on a normal treadmill: the slat belt you can drive yourself. Sole’s C80 is a curved, self-powered slat treadmill. Sole’s listing, a retailer listing and independent coverage agree on a 62 by 19-inch slat belt, eight levels of magnetic resistance, a 330 lb maximum user weight and a lifetime frame warranty, and Sole lists it at 261 lb. On a curved deck you set the pace by where you stand, and the resistance makes the belt heavier for pushes. What it cannot do, compared with the Tread+ Vision: it has no motor, so no powered pace, incline or class-controlled speed, and no screen, camera or Run Analysis; you can follow the Peloton App on a phone or tablet beside it, but nothing changes the belt for you. Its warranty is lifetime on the frame and one year on the deck, electronics, parts and labour. Sole listed it at $2,399.99 on 5 November 2026, about a third of the Tread+ Vision’s $6,695 before assembly.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Brands", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/brands/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Peloton Tread", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/peloton-tread/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between the Peloton Tread and Tread+?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The current Tread Vision has a conventional 59 by 20-inch belt, 12.5% incline and costs $3,495. The Tread+ Vision has a 67 by 20-inch slat belt, 15% incline, Free Mode and Sled Mode, weighs 460 lb and costs $6,695 plus a required $299 assembly fee. Both have the same 23.8-inch screen, camera, top speed and 300 lb user limit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Peloton Tread+ still sold?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Peloton relaunched the Tread+ in December 2023 after the 2021 recall, and on 1 October 2026 replaced it with the Tread+ Vision at $6,695 in the US and Canada. The previous Cross Training Tread+ is no longer sold directly by Peloton.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use a Peloton Tread without a membership?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Partly. Peloton says the Just Run feature on the Tread Vision does not need an All-Access Membership, so you can set speed and incline manually and see basic metrics. Classes, programmes and the Peloton IQ features need the membership. Peloton briefly removed Just Run for non-members in 2021 before restoring it, so check the current terms.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much is the Peloton membership for the Tread?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Peloton raised the US All-Access Membership from $44 to $49.99 a month in October 2025, according to coverage at the time. Peloton's membership page did not show a price when we checked on 20 October 2026, so confirm the current fee at checkout. One membership covers the household.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Peloton Tread safe after the recall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Tread+ was recalled in 2021 after a child died, and a breakaway rear guard was approved as the fix in May 2023; Tread Lock, a passcode lock, was added to all Treads. We found no Peloton treadmill recall on cpsc.gov after 2021. Ask Peloton to confirm the rear protection on a current Tread+ Vision, and keep children and pets away from any treadmill.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much space does a Peloton Tread need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Tread Vision measures 68 by 33 inches, but Peloton asks for 24 inches clear on each side and in front and 78.7 inches behind, and a ceiling at least 20 inches above the user's height on its specification page. All three models need a dedicated, grounded 15-amp circuit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a used Peloton Tread worth buying?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can be, if the recall remedy is confirmed and the price reflects a used-equipment activation fee, the cost of moving a 290 lb-plus machine and the lack of a transferred warranty. Peloton's refurbished store listed no Treads when we checked, and the previous Cross Training Tread needs to cost well under the $2,195 Tread Flex to make sense.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-20",
      dateModified: "2026-10-20",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 3,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/peloton-tread/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/peloton-tread/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "brands");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-20",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-20",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "peloton treadmill",
      "peloton tread",
      "peloton tread vs tread+",
      "peloton tread flex",
      "peloton tread vision",
      "peloton tread recall",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Closest on Amazon: NordicTrack Commercial 2450", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/brands/">Brands</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buyer’s Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Peloton Treadmill: Tread vs Tread+, Costs and Recalls</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Peloton replaced its whole treadmill line on 1 October 2026. This guide sets out what the Tread Flex, Tread Vision and Tread+ Vision cost delivered, what the membership adds and what the machine does without it, what changed after the 2021 recalls, and who would be better served by a different treadmill.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 20, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#nordictrack-commercial-2450"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31NVjoFCMQL._SL500_.jpg" alt="NordicTrack Commercial 2450" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#peloton-tread-vision"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31jcwMrxC5L._SL500_.jpg" alt="Peloton Tread Vision" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#sole-c80-slat-alternative"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31bmqOOpE8L._SL500_.jpg" alt="Sole C80 Curved Manual Treadmill" width="500" height="333" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>NordicTrack Commercial 2450 · Peloton Tread Vision · Sole C80 Curved Manual Treadmill</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Searches for a Peloton treadmill have climbed through 2026, and anyone who last looked a
          year ago will find a different lineup. On 1 October Peloton stopped selling its Cross
          Training Tread and Tread+ directly and replaced them with three machines: the folding
          Tread Flex, the Tread Vision and the slat-belt Tread+ Vision. They share one thing that
          matters more than any specification — they are built to be used with a paid All-Access
          Membership. This guide works through what each costs once it is in your house, what you
          get with and without the membership, how the 2021 recalls changed the machines, what
          space and power they need, whether a used Tread is a sensible buy, and when a different
          treadmill is the better use of the money.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Closest on Amazon:</span> <a href="#nordictrack-commercial-2450">NordicTrack Commercial 2450</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Most Buyers:</span> <a href="#peloton-tread-vision">Peloton Tread Vision</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Closest on Amazon:</span> <a href="#sole-c80-slat-alternative">Sole C80 Curved Manual Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          20 October 2026 we checked Peloton's US pages for the Tread Flex, Tread Vision and Tread+
          Vision (product, shop and specification pages), its membership page, which did not
          display a membership price when we looked, its refurbished-equipment page, its
          22 September 2026 launch press release and its fiscal 2026 results. We cross-checked the
          figures against John Lewis's Tread Vision listing, launch coverage from Pelo Buddy, The
          Gadgeteer and The Clip Out, and Precor's US site, and we read the US Consumer Product
          Safety Commission's two 2021 Peloton treadmill recall notices, its 2022 and 2023
          updates and its January 2023 civil-penalty announcement. Figures on the product cards
          are ones on which Peloton and at least one independent source agree; where sources, or
          Peloton's own pages, disagree we say so. We have not tested any Peloton treadmill.
          Prices are what onepeloton.com showed on the day we checked (20 Oct 2026) and change
          often. Peloton sells the Tread Flex and Tread+ Vision on its own site rather than on Amazon,
          so the first card shows the nearest folding class-screen treadmill Amazon sells, the
          NordicTrack Commercial 2450, with the figures checked for our heavy-duty treadmill guide;
          Amazon sells no motorised slat-belt treadmill, so the second card shows the nearest Amazon
          machine to the Tread+ Vision's slat belt and Free Mode, Sole's self-powered C80, with the
          figures checked for our Sole treadmill guide; the text below still covers the Tread+ Vision
          itself, which you buy from Peloton. As an Amazon Associate we earn from qualifying purchases.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">Should You Buy a Peloton Treadmill? The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#lineup">The Current Peloton Treads</a></li>
            <li><a href="#tread-vs-tread-plus">Tread vs Tread+</a></li>
            <li><a href="#delivered-cost">What It Costs Delivered</a></li>
            <li><a href="#membership">Membership and Just Run</a></li>
            <li><a href="#recalls">The 2021 Recalls and What Changed</a></li>
            <li><a href="#space-and-delivery">Space, Weight and Delivery</a></li>
            <li><a href="#used-and-refurbished">Used and Refurbished Treads</a></li>
            <li><a href="#peloton-and-precor">Peloton and Precor: Who Makes What</a></li>
            <li><a href="#alternatives">Who Should Buy Something Else</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Should You Buy a Peloton Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy a Peloton treadmill only if you already know that instructor-led Peloton
            classes are what will keep you running, and you are prepared to pay for the All-Access
            Membership for as long as you own it.</strong> For that buyer the Tread Vision, at
            $3,495 on Peloton's site on the day we checked, is the sensible default; the folding
            Tread Flex, at $2,195, is the choice if floor space decides it; and the slat-belt
            Tread+ Vision, at $6,695 plus a compulsory $299 assembly fee, is for a small group of
            runners who specifically want a slat belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            For everyone else the arithmetic usually points elsewhere. Peloton's classes also run
            on the Peloton App, on a phone, tablet or television beside any treadmill, and a
            machine that works fully without a subscription costs the same in its fifth year as
            in its first. Our <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs Peloton comparison</a> explains how the two big platforms differ in what a session feels like.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three things are worth knowing before you go further. Peloton's lineup changed on
            1 October 2026, so most reviews you will find describe machines Peloton no longer sells
            directly. The Tread+ was the subject of one of the most serious treadmill recalls of
            recent years, and the current model's safety arrangements are worth confirming in
            writing. And Peloton's own pages disagree with each other on several practical details
            — the warranty, the assembly fee and the ceiling height — which we set out below rather
            than pick one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Current Peloton Treads: Flex, Vision and Tread+ Vision</h2>
<p>
            Peloton announced the three machines on 22 September 2026 and put them on sale in the US
            and Canada on 1 October. According to launch coverage, the Cross Training Tread and
            Cross Training Tread+ are no longer sold directly by Peloton, and there is no trade-in
            programme for existing owners. The table gives the figures from Peloton's US
            specification page on 20 October 2026; the product cards below carry only the ones an
            independent source also confirms.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Peloton figure</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tread Flex</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tread Vision</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tread+ Vision</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,195</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,495</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$6,695</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">59 x 20 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">59 x 20 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">67 x 20 in. slats</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0-12.5%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0-12.5%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0-15%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Top speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12.5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12.5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12.5 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Screen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">21.5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">23.8 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">23.8 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Camera</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Machine weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">290 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">316 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">460 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">User limits</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700" colspan="3">105-300 lb, 4 ft 11 in to 6 ft 4 in, age 16 and over, on all three</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two of those cells are disputed elsewhere. One US retailer lists the Tread Flex at a
            400 lb user limit rather than Peloton's 300 lb, and the same retailer gives the Tread+
            Vision 12.5% incline rather than 15%. John Lewis's UK listing gives the Tread Vision
            0 to 12% incline. In each case we would go by Peloton's US figure and the label on the
            machine, but we have left the disputed numbers off the cards.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The motor figures</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton's product pages do not quote horsepower. Its specification page does — 2 HP DC
            for the Flex, 3 HP AC for the Vision and 2 HP AC for the Tread+ Vision — and John
            Lewis's listing agrees on 3 HP for the Vision. Peloton does not say whether these are
            continuous-duty ratings, so they cannot be compared directly with another brand's CHP
            figure. The motor warranty term, discussed below, tells you more.
          </p>
        </div>
