        <div id="tread-vs-tread-plus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Tread vs Tread+: Is the Slat Belt Worth Almost Double?</h2>
<p>
            For most people, no. The Tread Vision and Tread+ Vision share a screen, a camera, the
            Run Analysis feature, the class library, the top speed and the user limits. What the
            extra $3,200 or so buys is the deck, and a slat belt is a genuinely different thing
            from a conventional belt rather than a better version of it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the Tread+ Vision adds</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A slat belt.</strong> The running surface is 59 rubberised slats on a track rather than a belt sliding over a deck. Peloton describes it as softer and energy-returning; slat belts are also the design used on many high-end commercial treadmills.</li>
            <li><strong>A longer running surface.</strong> 67 inches against 59, which matters most to <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>tall runners</a> with a long stride at speed.</li>
            <li><strong>More incline.</strong> 15% on Peloton's figures, against 12.5%.</li>
            <li><strong>Free Mode and Sled Mode.</strong> Free Mode turns the motor off so your feet drive the belt; Sled Mode adds resistance for pushing, which Peloton gives as up to 300 lb in one place and 350 lb in another.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What it costs you</h3>
          <p>
            Beyond the price, the Tread+ Vision is 460 lb and 75 by 37 inches, needs an 11-inch step
            up, and cannot be self-assembled. It does not raise the user limits: the weight ceiling
            is still 300 lb and the height ceiling 6 ft 4 in, so a heavier or taller runner gains
            nothing on that front. If what attracts you is the slat belt itself rather than Peloton,
            our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a> covers the manual slat-belt machines that do the same job without a motor or a screen.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where the Tread Flex fits</h3>
          <p>
            The Flex is the Tread Vision minus the camera and with a smaller screen, on a frame
            that folds. It keeps the 59-inch belt, the 12.5 mph top speed and the 12.5% incline.
            If you do not care about Run Analysis and the Tread has to share a room, it saves
            $1,300 against the Vision.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A note on Run Analysis</h3>
          <p>
            Run Analysis uses the camera to measure cadence, vertical oscillation and stride length
            and give a running efficiency score with tips. It is a coaching feature, not an injury
            or medical assessment. This site reviews equipment and does not give medical advice; if
            you are running through pain or returning from an injury, a physical therapist who can
            watch you run in person is the right source of form advice, not a treadmill camera.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivered-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What a Peloton Tread Costs Delivered, and Over Three Years</h2>
<p>
            The list price is the smallest part of the decision, because the membership runs for
            as long as you want the machine to do what you bought it for. These are the figures
            Peloton's US site showed on 20 October 2026, before sales tax; they change often, and
            Peloton runs frequent promotions.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Item</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tread Flex</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tread Vision</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tread+ Vision</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hardware</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,195</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,495</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$6,695</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Delivery</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free with self-assembly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free with self-assembly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Included in assembly</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Expert assembly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$150 or $250 (page conflicts)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$150</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$299, required</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Delivered, assembled</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,345-$2,445</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,645</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$6,994</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Plus 36 months at $49.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $4,145-$4,245</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $5,445</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $8,794</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The membership line needs a caveat. Peloton raised the US All-Access fee from $44 to
            $49.99 a month in October 2025, according to coverage of that announcement at the time.
            Peloton's membership page did not display a price when we checked, so we have not
            verified that $49.99 is still the rate today. Treat the bottom row as an illustration
            of scale — roughly $1,800 over three years — and confirm the current fee at checkout.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Other costs that turn up</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Haulaway.</strong> Peloton offers removal of old equipment through Taskrabbit at a $155 member rate.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns.</strong> The 30-day home trial is for first-time buyers in the 48 contiguous states, one per household, and Peloton's footnote says return fees apply without stating the amount. Ask for the figure before you order.</li>
            <li><strong>Protection plan.</strong> Peloton sells an optional plan extending cover to 48 months in total; the price was not shown on the pages we checked.</li>
            <li><strong>Electrical work.</strong> All three need a dedicated, grounded circuit rated for 15 amps or more. If the room you have in mind does not have one, an electrician's visit belongs in the budget.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The warranty, read carefully</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton's pages describe the warranty in two ways. The product sections say a 12-month
            limited warranty covers the touchscreen, frame, drive motor, belt and most components.
            The FAQs and comparison tables add a three-year belt and motor guarantee and a
            five-year frame guarantee on the Flex and Vision. On the Tread+ Vision page, one section
            says three years for the belt and motor and another says five. Get the terms for your
            model in writing with the order. Either way they are shorter than the lifetime frame
            and motor cover common on subscription-free treadmills at the same price, and Peloton
            says moving the unit outside the US voids the Tread+ Vision warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What All-Access Adds, and What the Tread Does Without It</h2>
<p>
            Peloton states that an All-Access Membership is required to access all Peloton content
            and applicable features on its hardware. One membership covers the whole household,
            with a profile for each person, and covers one of each Peloton product type in the home.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">With the membership</h3>
          <p>
            You get the live and on-demand class library — Peloton's launch release put it at more
            than 17,000 Tread classes — plus scenic and training programmes, leaderboards and the
            newer Peloton IQ features: personalised pace targets, heart-rate zone workouts using a
            watch or strap, ReRuns that replay the incline profile of an outdoor route, and Run
            Analysis on the camera models. Launch coverage notes that most of the IQ features need
            All-Access.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Without the membership: Just Run</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton's Tread Vision shop page states that you do not need an All-Access Membership to
            use the Just Run feature, which lets you set speed and incline manually with basic
            metrics on screen. That is what you are left with: a treadmill with a large screen that
            shows your numbers, and without the classes, programmes or IQ features that the price is
            built around.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is worth knowing that this has not always been true. In May 2021 Peloton's recall
            software update briefly made Just Run unavailable to owners without a membership; after
            complaints, Peloton restored it and said all Tread owners could use Tread Lock and Just
            Run. That episode is the clearest illustration of the general risk with any
            subscription-led machine: what it does without the subscription is decided by software,
            and software can change.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The cheaper route to the same classes</h3>
          <p>
            The Peloton App, on a tablet or television, carries the class library to any treadmill.
            You follow the instructor's speed and incline cues on your own console rather than
            having them shown on Peloton's screen, and features that depend on Peloton hardware are
            limited. For many people that is most of what they wanted from Peloton for a fraction of
            the outlay. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills without subscriptions</a> lists machines whose consoles work fully on their own, which pair naturally with the app.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The 2021 Recalls and What Changed</h2>
<p>
            Peloton recalled both of its treadmills on 5 May 2021, and the details matter if you are
            buying a used one or simply want to know how the current machines got to where they are.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Tread+ recall (CPSC 21-128)</h3>
          <p>
            The US Consumer Product Safety Commission recalled about 125,000 Tread+ treadmills,
            model TR01, because adult users, children, pets and objects could be pulled under the
            rear of the machine. A six-year-old child died after being pulled under it, and at the
            time of the recall Peloton had received 72 reports of people, pets or objects being
            pulled under, including 29 reports of injuries to children. The TR01 had been sold since
            September 2018, at first as the Peloton Tread, and was renamed Tread+ in September 2020;
            it sold for about $4,295. The recall followed a CPSC safety warning in April 2021 that
            Peloton had publicly disputed.
          </p>
          <p>
            In January 2023 Peloton agreed to pay a $19,065,000 civil penalty to settle the CPSC's
            charges that it failed to report the hazard immediately and that it distributed 38
            recalled Tread+ treadmills after the recall was announced. By the time Peloton reported
            to the agency, the CPSC said, there had been more than 150 reports of pull-under
            incidents.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Tread recall (CPSC 21-129)</h3>
          <p>
            The smaller recall covered about 1,050 of the then-new Peloton Tread, model TR02, in the
            US and about 5,400 in Canada, sold from November 2020 to March 2021 for about $2,495.
            The touchscreen could loosen and fall; Peloton knew of 18 reports of it loosening and
            six of it detaching, with no US injuries. The remedy was a refund or a free service visit
            to secure the screen. This recall is sometimes forgotten in second-hand listings, so do
            not assume a regular Tread from that period was unaffected.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What changed</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Tread Lock.</strong> A software lock that requires a four-digit passcode to start the belt. Peloton's set-up guidance says it locks again after 45 seconds of inactivity outside a workout, and every profile on the machine shares the same code; some 2021 coverage reported 30 seconds.</li>
            <li><strong>The rear guard.</strong> On 18 May 2023 the CPSC announced an approved repair for the Tread+: a breakaway rear guard that pivots away when it touches a person or object, cutting power to the unit and slowing the belt. It was free, installed professionally, for owners who registered. The CPSC said that by then Peloton had received 351 pull-under reports in total, including the child's death and 90 injury reports.</li>
            <li><strong>Refunds.</strong> Full refunds ran until 6 November 2023, with prorated refunds after that.</li>
            <li><strong>The relaunched Tread+.</strong> Peloton put the Tread+ back on sale in December 2023, and new orders came with the rear guard fitted, according to coverage at the time.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Peloton's current Tread+ Vision pages did not mention the rear guard when we checked.
            We would expect it on a current slat-belt Peloton, but ask Peloton to confirm in writing
            what rear protection the machine has before you order. On cpsc.gov we found no Peloton
            treadmill recall after 2021; Peloton's later recalls were of Bike seat posts in 2023 and
            2025. Whatever the model, keep children and pets away from any running treadmill,
            remove the safety key after each session and use Tread Lock.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Space, Weight, Power and Delivery</h2>
<p>
            A Peloton Tread is a heavy, tall machine, and Peloton's clearance figures are larger
            than many buyers expect. Measure for all of them before you order.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Floor space and clearance</h3>
          <p>
            In use, the Flex measures 70.5 by 33.6 inches, the Vision 68 by 33 inches and the Tread+
            Vision 75 by 37 inches. Peloton asks for 24 inches clear on the left, right and front
            and 78.7 inches — about six and a half feet — behind. Add those up and the Vision needs a
            clear rectangle of roughly 14 feet by 7 feet; the rear clearance is a safety margin,
            not a suggestion. Folded, the Flex comes down to 37.8 inches long, but the clearance
            still applies whenever it is unfolded.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ceiling height</h3>
          <p>
            Here Peloton's own pages disagree. The specification page says the ceiling should be at
            least 20 inches above the user's height. The Vision and Flex shop pages say at least
            seven feet and recommend about 24 inches above your head, and the Tread+ Vision FAQ says
            24 inches above the user. By the 20-inch rule a 6 ft runner needs 7 ft 8 in, and
            because the rule does not say whether it counts the 8-inch step-up of the Flex and
            Vision, adding it to be safe brings that to 8 ft 4 in; on the Tread+ Vision's 11-inch
            deck, more. Use the most demanding figure for the tallest person in the house, and remember
            that incline lifts the front of the deck.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Weight, floors and stairs</h3>
          <p>
            The machines weigh 290 lb, 316 lb and 460 lb. Peloton says the heaviest box for the Flex
            is 320 lb and for the Vision 273 lb, and self-assembly takes two people — up to an hour
            for the Flex and up to two hours for the Vision. Peloton asks for a hard, flat, stable
            floor and advises against high-pile carpet; a garage or unfinished basement is
            acceptable, but it recommends a temperature-controlled room. If the route in involves a
            narrow staircase or an upstairs room, ask before ordering whether the delivery team will
            take it there.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton specifies a dedicated, grounded circuit rated for 15 amps or more on all three,
            at 120 V. A dedicated circuit means nothing else of note on that breaker, so a room
            shared with a space heater or a window air-conditioner may need rewiring. Do not run any
            treadmill from an ordinary extension lead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-and-refurbished" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Used or Previous-Generation Peloton Tread</h2>
<p>
            The new lineup has made the second-hand market more interesting, because the previous
            Cross Training Tread is now being sold off and there are years of earlier Treads in
            circulation. There are three routes, each with its own catch.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Previous-generation stock</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton listed the Cross Training Tread at $3,295 before the change. Pelo Buddy reported
            it on Amazon at $2,295 during the 6 and 7 October 2026 sale; that price has ended and we
            have not confirmed what it sells for now. A new previous-generation Tread still comes
            with a manufacturer warranty and receives software features its hardware supports, but
            it has no Run Analysis camera, and its price needs to sit well below the $2,195 Tread
            Flex to make sense.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Certified refurbished</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton's refurbished store listed only the Bike and Bike+ when we checked, not any
            Tread. If you see a "refurbished Peloton Tread" elsewhere, it has been refurbished by
            someone other than Peloton, and you should ask who did the work and what warranty
            comes with it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Private sale and Repowered</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton charges a one-time used-equipment activation fee when a new member activates
            second-hand hardware. It was $95 when it was introduced in 2024, Peloton waived it until
            31 March 2026, and at the 2025 launch of Repowered, Peloton's own resale marketplace, it
            was reported at $45 for purchases made there. Reports at that launch also said Repowered
            did not support the Tread+. Check the fee that applies on the day, and add it to the
            asking price.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to check on a used Tread</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Recall status first.</strong> For a Tread+ (TR01), ask for proof that the rear guard was fitted and give Peloton the serial number to confirm. For a 2020-21 Tread (TR02), confirm the screen fix was done. Federal law prohibits selling products subject to a recall, so a seller who cannot show the remedy is a reason to walk away.</li>
            <li><strong>The account.</strong> Make sure the seller has removed the machine from their account so it can be activated in yours.</li>
            <li><strong>The move.</strong> At 290 lb or more, a Tread needs at least two people and the right lifting points. Price a professional mover into the deal.</li>
            <li><strong>The warranty.</strong> Assume none transfers unless Peloton confirms otherwise.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> covers the mechanical checks — belt, motor noise, incline through its full range — that
            apply to any second-hand machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="peloton-and-precor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Peloton and Precor: Who Makes What</h2>
<p>
            Peloton owns Precor, the commercial gym-equipment maker. It bought Precor from Amer
            Sports for $420 million in a deal completed on 1 April 2021. In 2022 Peloton explored
            selling it but did not go ahead, and Precor was later folded into a commercial business
            unit alongside Peloton's own gym products. Peloton's fiscal 2026 results, published on
            6 August 2026, credited equipment sales across both the Peloton and Precor brands, and
            said the commercial unit grew by double digits.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a home buyer the connection is mostly background. Precor's US site lists its
            treadmills as commercial products, including a slat-belt model, alongside a Peloton
            Commercial Series treadmill and curated Peloton running and walking classes for Precor
            machines. The CPSC recall database lists a 1998 recall that included some <a href="/precor-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Precor
            treadmill</a> models and a 2025 recall of a Precor cable machine, but no recent Precor
            treadmill recall.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Peloton Treads themselves are consumer machines, and John Lewis's listing gives the
            Tread Vision's country of origin as Taiwan, where the 2021 CPSC notices also said the
            recalled models were made. If what you want is gym-grade hardware rather than Peloton
            classes, our <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmill guide</a> explains what you pay for and whether it makes sense at home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            A Peloton Tread is a good fit for a narrow buyer and an expensive mismatch for several
            others. These are the cases where we would look elsewhere first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You want the treadmill to drive the workout for you.</strong> Peloton
            instructors tell you what speed and incline to set; iFIT on NordicTrack and <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>ProForm
            treadmills</a> can change them automatically along filmed outdoor routes, on a much wider
            range of machines and prices. Our <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart treadmill roundup</a> sets the Tread Vision against iFIT, JRNY and open-app machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You are not sure you will keep paying.</strong> A treadmill with a complete
            console and open Bluetooth, used with the Peloton App or Zwift when you want them, keeps
            its value whether or not you subscribe. The Tread without a membership is a
            screen-heavy <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>manual treadmill</a> with a short warranty for its price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You weigh more than 300 lb or are taller than 6 ft 4 in.</strong> Every current
            Peloton Tread is outside its stated limits for you, and several subscription-free
            treadmills are rated higher with 22-inch-wide decks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You want long warranty cover.</strong> Twelve months limited, with three to five
            years on the belt, motor and frame, is short against the lifetime frame and motor cover
            that many rivals at $2,000 to $3,500 offer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You mainly walk.</strong> At walking speeds, the Tread's running features add
            little, and a well-made folding treadmill or a <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pad</a> costs a fraction of the
            price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Peloton if</strong> the classes are what get you on the belt, you will
            keep the membership, you fit the limits and you have the room and the circuit. In that
            case the Tread Vision is the one to buy, the Flex if it must fold, and the Tread+ Vision
            only if a slat belt matters enough to pay roughly double.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Peloton Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Most disappointing Peloton purchases go wrong before the machine arrives. These are the four we would avoid.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Budgeting for the hardware and not the membership</h3>
              <p>A $3,495 Tread Vision becomes roughly $5,400 over three years once a membership at the October 2025 rate of $49.99 is added, and the machine does little more than a manual treadmill without it. Before ordering, price three years of ownership at the fee shown at checkout, and compare that total with a subscription-free treadmill plus the Peloton App.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a used Tread+ without proof of the recall fix</h3>
              <p>The 2021 Tread+ recall covered about 125,000 machines, and a used TR01 without the approved rear guard carries the pull-under hazard the recall was about. Get the serial number, ask Peloton to confirm the remedy, and walk away if the seller cannot show the guard was fitted. Check that a 2020-21 Tread had its screen secured as well.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring the floor but not the clearance, ceiling or circuit</h3>
              <p>Peloton asks for 78.7 inches behind the deck, 24 inches at the sides and front, a ceiling at least 20 inches above the user, and a dedicated 15-amp circuit. Measure all four for the tallest user before you order, and book an electrician first if the room has no free circuit.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Treating the 30-day home trial as risk-free</h3>
              <p>Peloton’s trial is limited to first-time buyers in the 48 contiguous states, and its own footnote says return fees apply without stating them. Ask Peloton for the return fee for your model in writing before you pay, so you know what changing your mind would cost.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A Peloton treadmill makes sense for someone who already knows
          Peloton classes are what get them running and will keep paying for the membership; for
          that buyer the $3,495 Tread Vision is the default.</strong> The $2,195 Tread Flex is the
          choice if it must fold, and the $6,695 Tread+ Vision, with its required $299 assembly, is
          only for runners set on a slat belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            Budget for about three years of membership, measure for the 78.7-inch rear clearance and a
          dedicated circuit, get the warranty and return fee in writing, and confirm the recall
          remedy on any used Tread+. If you are less certain about Peloton, the app on a
          subscription-free machine is the cheaper way in — our <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">smart treadmill roundup</a> sets out the alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

