        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the Peloton Tread and Tread+?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The current Tread Vision has a conventional 59 by 20-inch belt, 12.5% incline and costs $3,495. The Tread+ Vision has a 67 by 20-inch slat belt, 15% incline, Free Mode and Sled Mode, weighs 460 lb and costs $6,695 plus a required $299 assembly fee. Both have the same 23.8-inch screen, camera, top speed and 300 lb user limit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Peloton Tread+ still sold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Peloton relaunched the Tread+ in December 2023 after the 2021 recall, and on 1 October 2026 replaced it with the Tread+ Vision at $6,695 in the US and Canada. The previous Cross Training Tread+ is no longer sold directly by Peloton.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use a Peloton Tread without a membership?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Partly. Peloton says the Just Run feature on the Tread Vision does not need an All-Access Membership, so you can set speed and incline manually and see basic metrics. Classes, programmes and the Peloton IQ features need the membership. Peloton briefly removed Just Run for non-members in 2021 before restoring it, so check the current terms.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is the Peloton membership for the Tread?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Peloton raised the US All-Access Membership from $44 to $49.99 a month in October 2025, according to coverage at the time. Peloton's membership page did not show a price when we checked on 20 October 2026, so confirm the current fee at checkout. One membership covers the household.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Peloton Tread safe after the recall?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The Tread+ was recalled in 2021 after a child died, and a breakaway rear guard was approved as the fix in May 2023; Tread Lock, a passcode lock, was added to all Treads. We found no Peloton treadmill recall on cpsc.gov after 2021. Ask Peloton to confirm the rear protection on a current Tread+ Vision, and keep children and pets away from any treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much space does a Peloton Tread need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The Tread Vision measures 68 by 33 inches, but Peloton asks for 24 inches clear on each side and in front and 78.7 inches behind, and a ceiling at least 20 inches above the user's height on its specification page. All three models need a dedicated, grounded 15-amp circuit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a used Peloton Tread worth buying?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, if the recall remedy is confirmed and the price reflects a used-equipment activation fee, the cost of moving a 290 lb-plus machine and the lack of a transferred warranty. Peloton's refurbished store listed no Treads when we checked, and the previous Cross Training Tread needs to cost well under the $2,195 Tread Flex to make sense.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
